{"word": "torture", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "torture", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈtoɹt͡ʃɚ/", "tags": ["General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈtɔːt͡ʃə(ɹ)/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}], "forms": [{"word": "tortures", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "The infliction of severe pain or anguish, especially as an interrogation technique or punishment; (usually in the plural) a technique, method, or device which is designed to inflict such anguish.", "tags": ["countable", "uncountable"], "examples": ["People confess to anything under torture.", "Using large dogs to attack bound, hand-cuffed prisoners is clearly torture.", "What new tortures await me?"], "quotes": [{"text": "If Cheney calls it enhanced interrogation, he argues, this still doesn’t change the meaning of the word torture, which Cheney and the public know perfectly well. But cognitive linguists like Lakoff (1996) remind us that the public can be manipulated into believing that torture is “merely” an enhanced interrogation technique and thus does not protest.", "reference": "2014 January 25, Claire Kramsch, “Language and Culture”, in AILA Review, volume 27, number 5, John Benjamins, →DOI, →ISSN, page 35:"}, {"text": "Santorum, in a comment regarding Senator John McCain's repudiation of torture, stated, \"He doesn't understand how enhanced interrogation works. I mean, you break somebody, and after they've broken they become cooperative\" (Summers 2011).", "reference": "2015 November 30, Shane O'Mara, Why Torture Doesn’t Work: The Neuroscience of Interrogation, Harvard University Press, →ISBN, page 12:"}], "synonyms": ["enhanced interrogation techniques"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": [{"definition": "(BDSM, in combination) Sexual activity involving the infliction of pain to a certain body part or in a certain manner.", "tags": ["countable", "in compounds", "uncountable"], "examples": ["cock and ball torture", "tickle torture"], "quotes": [], "synonyms": ["enhanced interrogation techniques"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}]}, {"definition": "Severe pain or anguish, of mind or body.", "tags": ["countable", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": ["anguish", "rend", "difficulty", "distress", "grief", "hardship", "pain", "smart", "torment", "torture", "trouble", "misery", "pain", "wark"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(in figurative or extended use) An unpleasant sensation or its infliction: embarrassment, heartache, etc.", "tags": ["countable", "uncountable"], "examples": ["Every time she says 'goodbye' it is torture!", "Coventry City midfielder Josh Ruffels described his 11 months out injured as 'absolute torture' after the goalless draw with Derby County Under-21s. (http://www.ccfc.co.uk/news/article/eleven-months-of-absolute-torture-760652.aspx)"], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["enhanced interrogation", "torture", "torment", "excruciate"], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈtoɹt͡ʃɚ/", "tags": ["General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈtɔːt͡ʃə(ɹ)/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}], "forms": [{"word": "tortures", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "torturing", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "tortured", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "tortured", "tags": ["past"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(transitive) To intentionally inflict severe pain or suffering on (someone), usually with the aim of forcing confessions or punishing them.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": ["1 August 2014, Barack Obama, \"Press Conference by the President\"; transcript published online by the Obama White House Archives, 